# toolbox-db

Claude Code plugin that turns the tools of an [MCP Toolbox](https://mcp-toolbox.dev) server into
native Claude Code tools, using Google's [`@toolbox-sdk/core`](https://github.com/googleapis/mcp-toolbox-sdk-js).

```
Claude Code ──stdio MCP──► mcp/server.mjs ──@toolbox-sdk/core──► Toolbox server ──► Postgres / MySQL / BigQuery / …
```

The plugin never touches database credentials. Those live in the Toolbox server's `tools.yaml`.
What the plugin needs — where the Toolbox server is and how to authenticate to it — comes from
**session environment variables**.

## Install

```bash
# in Claude Code
/plugin marketplace add ~/.dotfiles/config/claude/local-plugins
/plugin install toolbox-db@jofre-local
```

Node.js ≥ 18 is required. On first launch the MCP server runs `npm install` inside `mcp/`
(network needed once); set `TOOLBOX_AUTO_INSTALL=0` to do it yourself:

```bash
npm install --omit=dev --prefix ~/.dotfiles/config/claude/local-plugins/plugins/toolbox-db/mcp
```

## Session environment variables

Export these **before** starting Claude Code — the MCP server inherits the environment at launch.

| Variable | Default | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `TOOLBOX_URL` | `http://127.0.0.1:5000` | Base URL of the Toolbox server |
| `TOOLBOX_TOOLSET` | *(default toolset)* | Toolset name from `tools.yaml` |
| `TOOLBOX_TOOL_PREFIX` | *(none)* | Prefix added to every exposed tool name |
| `TOOLBOX_API_KEY` | — | Sent as `Authorization: Bearer …` to the Toolbox server |
| `TOOLBOX_GOOGLE_ID_TOKEN` | `0` | `1` → mint a Google ID token for `TOOLBOX_URL` (Cloud Run) |
| `TOOLBOX_CLIENT_HEADERS` | `{}` | JSON object of extra headers, e.g. `{"X-Env":"staging"}` |
| `TOOLBOX_AUTH_TOKENS` | `{}` | JSON map of Toolbox *auth service* → token, e.g. `{"my_auth":"eyJ…"}` |
| `TOOLBOX_AUTH_<SERVICE>` | — | Same thing, one var per service: `TOOLBOX_AUTH_MY_AUTH` → service `my_auth` |
| `TOOLBOX_BOUND_PARAMS` | `{}` | JSON map of parameters pinned on every call, e.g. `{"tenant_id":"42"}` |
| `TOOLBOX_PROTOCOL` | *(SDK default)* | MCP protocol version, e.g. `2025-06-18` |
| `TOOLBOX_STRICT` | `0` | `1` → SDK strict mode: fail the load if any auth token or bound param is unused |
| `TOOLBOX_AUTO_INSTALL` | `1` | `0` → never run `npm install` at startup |

fish:

```fish
set -gx TOOLBOX_URL http://127.0.0.1:5000
set -gx TOOLBOX_TOOLSET default
set -gx TOOLBOX_BOUND_PARAMS '{"tenant_id":"42"}'
```

`TOOLBOX_AUTH_TOKENS` wins over the per-variable form when both name the same service.

### Unused tokens and params

`@toolbox-sdk/core` rejects an entire toolset if any supplied auth token or bound param matches no
tool — easy to trip when the config lives in long-lived environment variables and you switch
toolsets. This bridge therefore probes the toolset first, applies only the tokens and params that
some tool actually uses, and reports the rest as warnings in `toolbox_status`. Set
`TOOLBOX_STRICT=1` to get the SDK's fail-loud behaviour back.

## What you get

Every tool in the loaded toolset, plus two built-ins:

- **`toolbox_status`** — resolved configuration (secrets redacted), loaded tools, warnings, last load error.
- **`toolbox_reload`** — re-fetch the toolset after `tools.yaml` changed; no Claude Code restart.

Slash commands: `/toolbox-db:setup`, `/toolbox-db:doctor`. A `toolbox-db` skill teaches Claude how
to use the tools safely (confirm writes, never fake SQL, how to extend `tools.yaml`).

If the Toolbox server is unreachable at startup the MCP server still comes up with the two
built-ins, so `toolbox_status` can explain what went wrong.

## Diagnostics

```bash
node ~/.dotfiles/config/claude/local-plugins/plugins/toolbox-db/mcp/doctor.mjs
```

## Layout

```
mcp/launch.mjs      entry point: dependency bootstrap, then server
mcp/server.mjs      MCP stdio server, Toolbox tool → MCP tool bridge
mcp/toolbox.mjs     @toolbox-sdk/core client construction and toolset loading
mcp/config.mjs      environment variable parsing + redacted description
mcp/bootstrap.mjs   first-run npm install
mcp/doctor.mjs      standalone diagnostics
```

## Limitations

- Credentials come from the environment only; there is no keychain or config-file support yet.
- Tool schemas are captured at load time — call `toolbox_reload` after changing `tools.yaml`.
- Toolbox tool output is passed through as text with no truncation.
